
  Run one /bin/sh line in each worktree named, or in every one with --all, from the worktree's
  root, several at once (--jobs, one per CPU by default). Everything after -- is the command.
  Without worktrees or a command, the wizard asks for them; your current worktree is picked first.

  Each command gets the environment of wtm-next, without the variables wtm sets in a worktree
  nor the ports run.toml declares; in an isolated worktree whose jobs run docker compose, it gets
  the worktree's own, as its hooks do. Its stdin is closed. Its output goes to a log under wtm's
  state, overwritten by the next run; a worktree that fails shows its last lines and its log.

  A failure does not stop the others: the run ends with the line that runs the command again
  where it did not pass. The exit code is 1 when any failed, whatever each command's own code.

  Usage:
    wtm-next exec [worktree...] [-- <command>] [flags]

  Examples:
    # Pick the worktrees, then type the command
    wtm exec

    wtm exec feat/login feat/signup -- pnpm test

    # Every worktree, two at a time, keeping each one's whole output
    wtm exec --all --jobs 2 --print -- git status --short

    # For a script or an agent, with the v2 JSON envelope
    wtm exec --all --yes --output json -- make lint

  Flags:
        --all                         Run in every worktree, the main one included
        --ask                         Ask again the questions this repository remembers an answer to, to change or forget it
    -n, --dry-run                     Show the plan and its fingerprint, apply nothing
        --expect-fingerprint string   Apply only if the facts still have this fingerprint, the one --dry-run printed
    -h, --help                        help for exec
        --jobs string                 How many worktrees run the command at once (default: one per CPU)
        --output string               Output format: text or json (default "text")
        --print                       Print each worktree's whole output, successes included
    -y, --yes                         Run unattended: no question, no confirmation (worktrees or --all, and the command after --, required)

  Global Flags:
    -q, --quiet   Silence human output; errors and the exit code are unaffected, and --output json still emits its document

